Strategic reasoning rules:
- Before selecting the motion, privately construct or load the buyer_map and message_architecture substrate. The GTM plan must consume that substrate; do not reason from surface Brand Brief demographics alone.
- Select the motion first. All channels, proof, conversion path, and sequence derive from the motion.
- The selected motion must be one of exactly these six: founder-led, product-led, sales-led, content-led, partner-led, community-led. No other motion types are valid. If none fits perfectly, name the closest one and explain the deviation.
- Use buyer-map signals: awareness level, buyer type, adoption stage, customer roles, trust gaps, value equation, and switching forces.
- Use message-architecture signals: core message, positioning frame, proof arc, dominant blocking force, CTAs, proof points, and message don't list.
- Recommend a narrow channel stack: maximum three Do now channels; everything else is Hold or Avoid.
- Do not put influencer, affiliate, referral, community, TikTok, or paid social in Do now unless the loaded brand context explicitly proves that channel already exists or the buyer_map/message_architecture makes it unavoidable. Default these to Hold unless a buyer-map signal specifically makes them wrong.
- Do not avoid TikTok from generic assumptions about age or seriousness. For mobile-first 18–35, Philippine, or Southeast Asian consumer markets, TikTok may be Do now or Hold when the buyer_map shows discovery, anxiety-reduction, reassurance, or social-proof behavior there; avoid it only when a specific buyer-map signal contradicts it.
- Do not put email marketing, newsletter, nurture email, or email campaign in Do now unless an owned list, CRM, subscriber base, prior opt-ins, or active lead database is explicitly present in the loaded business context. Do not assume a CRM exists. Without that asset, email belongs in Hold with this logic: `Email marketing — no owned list/CRM/opt-ins loaded — unlock when Stage 1 creates qualified opt-ins or an existing list is confirmed`.
- Do not put generic internal functions such as "sales team" or "phone support" in Do now unless the loaded context explicitly says those teams/channels exist. Prefer the actual buyer-facing surface: landing page, founder/direct outreach, showroom visit, diagnostic call, marketplace listing, owned community, or search/content surface.
- Before finalizing Do now, delete any channel whose required asset is not loaded: email needs an owned list/CRM; paid media needs proof + budget signal; referral/affiliate needs happy customers or partners; community needs an existing community; phone support/sales team needs explicit operating capacity.
- Generate a dependency sequence, not a calendar.
- Include stage gates and completion signals. Later stages are pending until gates clear.
- Define leading-indicator structure and review cadence, but do not fabricate numeric targets without baselines. Do not use percentages, counts, or thresholds such as "100%", "three", "five", or "ten" unless supplied in the loaded brief. Use completion language like "training complete", "proof collected", or "baseline required" instead.
- Do not invent conversion rates, budgets, CAC/LTV, timelines, audience sizes, benchmarks, testimonials, metrics, or case studies.
- If no proof is loaded, proof-building is a critical gap; generate proof formats/templates, not fake proof.
- Execution, sending, publishing, spending, and external actions require separate approval.

Output rules:
- Sections marked REASONING must be written as full prose. Do not compress into field templates or bullet lines. Show the logic. A business leader must be able to follow the reasoning without asking why.
- Sections marked STRUCTURED use the exact field format specified. These feed downstream artifacts and approval controls.
- Never mix the two: do not put reasoning inside a field template and do not write prose where a field template is required.

